=== layout: section

# Chapter 19: Cancer

=== layout: subsection

# 30. Tumor Microenvironment and Immune Evasion

=== layout: two-panels

# 30. Tumor Microenvironment

@left

- The tumor microenvironment (TME) includes vessels, fibroblasts, immune cells, matrix, and signals around cancer cells.
- How can each neighbor change tumor growth or treatment response?
- Why can low oxygen, acidity, and poor blood flow make a drug or an immune response less effective?

@right

![TME cell and matrix components](assets/set5_cancer/tme_components_2025.png)

=== layout: two-panels

# 30. A Changing Tumor Neighborhood

@left

- Leaky, disorganized vessels can deliver much more drug to one region than another.
- Fibroblasts deposit matrix that changes mechanics and immune-cell movement.
- Hypoxia can select stress-tolerant cells and alter immune and drug response.

@right

*Mechanistic prediction*

If a treatment needs T cells to enter a tumor, then a dense matrix, abnormal vessels, or suppressive cells can be as important as whether the cancer cell has the target molecule.

=== layout: two-panels

# 30. Immune Evasion Creates Barriers

@left

![TME-mediated immune evasion](assets/set5_cancer/tme_immune_evasion_2025.png)

@right

- A tumor can reduce antigen display, express PD-L1, recruit suppressive cells, or exclude T cells.
- "Hot" and "cold" describe immune context: hot tumors have more immune infiltration.
- PD-L1 staining and the location or abundance of CD8 T cells are examples of tissue measurements that can support a treatment hypothesis.

=== layout: one-panel

# 30. From Tissue to Evidence

@body

- A biomarker is useful only when the measurement, patient group, and decision are specified. A PD-L1 result is not a universal prediction of benefit.
- Checkpoint blockade works by releasing an inhibitory signal on T cells; it does not manufacture a tumor-specific immune response from nothing.
- Ask: *What barrier is visible? Which intervention is supposed to change it? What result would count as evidence?*

@citation

NCI, Tumor Microenvironment: https://www.cancer.gov/publications/dictionaries/cancer-terms/def/tumor-microenvironment

=== layout: subsection

# 31. CAR-T and CAR-NK Cell Therapies

=== layout: two-panels

# 31. CAR Recognition Design

@left

- A CAR binds a chosen marker outside the cell and activates the immune cell inside.
- What are the steps: collect, add CAR gene, expand, test, prepare, infuse?
- Why are CD19 in B-cell malignancies and BCMA in myeloma useful examples of a matched target and disease?

@right

![CAR antigen-binding, membrane, and signaling domains](assets/set5_cancer/car_receptor_2025.png)

=== layout: two-panels

# 31. Manufacturing Is Part of Therapy

@left

1. Collect or select immune cells.
2. Deliver the CAR DNA or RNA construct.
3. Expand the engineered population.
4. Test identity, sterility, viability, and activity.
5. Infuse and monitor the person, not merely the cells.

@right

- A cell product is variable biological material. Batch testing asks whether the intended cells were made; clinical follow-up asks what they do after infusion.
- Response can be measured with marrow testing, imaging, tumor markers, or a complete-remission endpoint appropriate to the disease.
- CAR-T and CAR-NK are distinct products; each needs product-specific safety and effectiveness evidence.

=== layout: two-panels

# 31. CAR-T and CAR-NK Compared

@left

![CAR prototype with a T-cell activation domain](assets/set5_cancer/car_receptor_2025.png)

@right

| Feature | CAR-T | CAR-NK |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Effector | T cell | NK cell |
| Added receptor | CAR | CAR |

The pictured receptor has a T-cell activation domain; CAR-NK constructs use product-specific signaling designs.

=== layout: one-panel

# 31. Why Solid Tumors Are Harder Targets

@body

- A good target should be present on tumor cells and limited on essential normal tissue. Few targets satisfy that perfectly.
- Engineered cells must traffic into the tumor, remain active in a suppressive TME, and avoid on-target damage to healthy cells that share the antigen.
- Cytokine-release syndrome and neurologic toxicity are important CAR-T risks. Fever, low blood pressure, confusion, or seizures require prompt clinical assessment; monitoring and management are part of therapy.

@citation

NCI, CAR T-Cell Therapy: https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/treatment/research/car-t-cells

=== layout: subsection

# 32. Liquid Biopsy and Minimal Residual Disease

=== layout: two-panels

# 32. What a Blood Sample Can Say

@left

- A liquid biopsy analyzes tumor material in fluid: often plasma ctDNA or circulating tumor cells.
- Which assay can detect a tumor-associated alteration? What would a positive result mean in this clinical setting?
- Why do blood volume, tumor shedding, sample timing, and assay depth affect detection?

@right

![Sampling tumor-derived material in blood](assets/set5_cancer/liquid_biopsy_workflow_crop.png)

=== layout: two-panels

# 32. MRD Is Near the Detection Limit

@left

## Follow a Trackable Signal

1. Identify a tumor-associated marker.
2. Sample at planned time points.
3. Compare the signal with the assay's detection limit.
4. Interpret the result with the clinical context.

@right

- MRD is very small amounts of cancer after treatment, measured by a specified assay.
- Blood-cancer MRD uses flow cytometry, molecular assays, or sequencing; ctDNA is a related solid-tumor research marker.
- A negative means "not detected by this assay now," not no cancer cells exist.

=== layout: two-panels

# 32. Signal, Noise, and Follow-Up

@left

*Possible signal*

- Tumor-specific variant falls after therapy.
- A targetable alteration is found.
- A rising tumor-associated signal prompts confirmatory evaluation.

@right

*Possible misleading result*

- Too little DNA was shed into blood.
- A single time point misses changing disease.
- Clonal hematopoiesis creates blood-cell DNA variants that are not from the tumor.

The next step is context, confirmation, and a decision rule - not treating one number as a diagnosis.

=== layout: one-panel

# 32. ctDNA and Circulating Tumor Cells

@body

- ctDNA is fragmented tumor-associated DNA; it can report variants but generally does not provide a living cell for functional study.
- Circulating tumor cells are intact, rare cells. They may support cell-level measurements, but finding and characterizing them is technically difficult.
- A liquid biopsy complements, rather than automatically replaces, a tissue biopsy: tissue supplies anatomy and can provide a larger, direct sample of the lesion.

@citation

NCI Dictionary, Liquid Biopsy: https://www.cancer.gov/publications/dictionaries/cancer-terms/def/liquid-biopsy

=== layout: subsection

# 33. Gene Therapy in Cancer

=== layout: two-panels

# 33. Genetic Functions That Attack Tumors

@left

- Cancer gene therapy changes a tumor or immune-cell function with delivered genetic material.
- How does this differ from replacing a missing inherited-disease gene?
- Which target is claimed: local injection, antigen, promoter, or viral replication?

@right

*Three design families*

1. Engineer immune cells to recognize a tumor marker.
2. Deliver a toxic or pro-apoptotic function preferentially to tumor cells.
3. Use an oncolytic virus that replicates preferentially in tumor cells and can stimulate immunity.

=== layout: two-panels

# 33. Enzyme, Prodrug, Cell Death

@left

![Engineered cell-killing gene](assets/set5_cancer/suicide_gene_2025.png)

This simplified diagram shows cell killing. The enzyme/prodrug route needs the additional drug-conversion step.

@right

- A delivered enzyme gene converts a later prodrug into toxin.
- Sequence: targeted delivery -> enzyme expression -> prodrug -> killing of transduced cells, sometimes with a local bystander effect.
- Delivery, expression, and off-target toxicity determine outcome; the vector does not kill every tumor cell.

=== layout: two-panels

# 33. Selectivity Is a Design Problem

@left

| Choice | Advantage | Test failure |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Local delivery | concentrate | coverage |
| Tumor promoter | restrict | healthy expression |
| Tumor antigen | focus | normal antigen |
| Viral replication | amplify | infection |

@right

- A biomarker may help select a person or a lesion, but it must be measured before claiming selectivity.
- Efficacy measures can include local response, tumor burden, duration of response, and survival; safety includes inflammatory and organ-specific toxicities.

=== layout: one-panel

# 33. Evidence for Cancer Gene Therapy

@body

- Separate a biological rationale from an established indication. A promising vector, gene, or animal result is not automatically a proven treatment.
- Compare local, regional, and systemic delivery by where the disease is, where the vector travels, and how adverse effects can be monitored.
- Oncolytic-virus therapy, engineered immune cells, and gene replacement use related tools but are not interchangeable categories.

@citation

NCI, Oncolytic Virus Therapy: https://www.cancer.gov/news-events/cancer-currents-blog/2018/oncolytic-viruses-to-treat-cancer

=== layout: subsection

# 34. Mutagenicity and Carcinogenicity

=== layout: two-panels

# 34. DNA Damage and Cancer Risk

@left

- A *mutagen* increases DNA sequence changes.
- A *carcinogen* increases the chance of cancer.
- How does the Ames test screen for mutagenic potential? Why is bacterial growth not a complete prediction of human cancer risk?

@right

- Some carcinogens are mutagens. Others change cancer risk through chronic inflammation, hormones, cell proliferation, or signaling without directly changing DNA letters.
- Exposure, dose, metabolism, tissue, and duration all matter.

=== layout: two-panels

# 34. Ames Test: A Reversion Experiment

@left

![Ames comparison: treated and control bacterial growth](assets/set5_cancer/ames_comparison_a_legacy.png)

@right

- Engineered bacteria cannot grow without a nutrient unless reverse mutation restores function.
- More revertant colonies after chemical exposure suggest mutagenic potential.
- An added liver-enzyme preparation can model metabolic activation of some compounds.

It is a screen, not a measure of human exposure, tissue specificity, or cancer incidence.

=== layout: two-panels

# 34. Hormone Signaling

@left

![Steroid hormone binds a receptor and changes gene expression](assets/set5_cancer/steroid_receptor_legacy.png)

@right

*A Different Route to Changed Cell Behavior*

- A hormone binds a receptor and changes transcription and protein production.
- Promoting cell division is different from directly creating a DNA mutation.
- A chemical can act through multiple mechanisms. This diagram alone cannot prove that a substance is nonmutagenic.

=== layout: one-panel

# 34. Mechanism Comparison

@body

| Mechanism | Mutagenic? | Cancer support |
| --- | --- | --- |
| DNA adduct or strand damage | often | creates heritable sequence changes |
| Repair error | often | makes a DNA change permanent |
| Hormonal signaling | not necessarily | increases division of susceptible cells |
| Chronic inflammation | not necessarily | damage and growth signaling |

Use the wording *hazard* for what an agent can do under some conditions and *risk* for the chance of harm at a particular exposure.

=== layout: subsection

# 35. Oncogenes and Tumor Suppressors

=== layout: two-panels

# 35. Genetic Changes That Drive Growth

@left

- How were oncogenes identified, and what experiment connects a DNA change to a transformed-cell phenotype?
- How do RAS, MYC, and BCR-ABL drive growth by different molecular routes?
- How can sequencing connect a tumor alteration to a possible targeted-treatment strategy?

@right

- A proto-oncogene has a normal controlled role in growth or survival.
- Gain-of-function, amplification, fusion, or inappropriate expression can create a driver.
- A tumor usually contains many changes; a detected alteration is not automatically the driver.

=== layout: two-panels

# 35. Finding an Oncogene

@left

![transfection assay used to identify transforming DNA](assets/set5_cancer/transfection_assay_legacy.png)

@right

- In a classic transfection experiment, DNA from a tumor is introduced into recipient cells.
- If cells transform, researchers isolate the responsible DNA and test it again.
- Transfer a candidate and observe phenotype: this distinguishes causation from correlation.

=== layout: big-image

@image

![35. Follow the Transforming DNA](assets/set5_cancer/transfection_assay_legacy.png)

@caption

Topic 35: Follow the Transforming DNA

=== layout: two-panels

# 35. Growth Drivers, Limits, and Repair

@left

*Oncogene*

- Usually activated or overexpressed.
- Can send a persistent grow/survive signal.
- Examples: RAS signaling, MYC transcriptional control, BCR-ABL fusion kinase.

@right

*Tumor suppressor or repair gene*

- Tumor suppressors help limit growth or promote apoptosis after damage.
- DNA-repair genes prevent mutations from accumulating.
- Loss can cooperate with an oncogene; "tumor suppressor" is not a synonym for every repair or death gene.

=== layout: two-panels

# 35. Inherited Cancer Risk

@left

![inherited-cancer-susceptibility visual](assets/set5_cancer/inherited_cancer_legacy.png)

@right

- An inherited variant can raise risk; more somatic changes may still be needed.
- Counseling considers variant, penetrance, family history, and prevention or screening.
- Do not treat a predisposition result as a diagnosis or a guarantee of cancer.

=== layout: big-image

@image

![35. A Growth Driver in a Longer Story](assets/set5_cancer/inherited_cancer_legacy.png)

@caption

Topic 35: A Growth Driver in a Longer Story

=== layout: subsection

# 36. Oncogenic and Oncolytic Viruses

=== layout: two-panels

# 36. Viruses and Cancer: Two Roles

@left

- What distinguishes an onco[*genic*]{color=red} virus from an onco[*lytic*]{color=red} virus?
- How can a virus contribute to cancer: persistent infection, altered host-cell control, chronic inflammation, or immune evasion?
- How might a virus be selected or engineered to replicate more effectively in tumor cells and kill them?

@right

They have distinct roles: oncogenic viruses can contribute to cancer; oncolytic viruses attack tumor cells. They are not simple opposite lytic/lysogenic categories.

=== layout: two-panels

# 36. Two Roles, Two Biological Questions

@left

![oncogenic-versus-oncolytic comparison](assets/set5_cancer/oncogenic_oncolytic_legacy.png)

@right

| Role | Main question |
| --- | --- |
| Oncogenic virus | How can infection raise cancer risk? |
| Oncolytic virus | Can it kill tumors safely? |

Vaccination can prevent particular causal infections; it does not treat every established tumor.

=== layout: big-image

@image

![36. Cancer-Causing and Cancer-Killing Viruses](assets/set5_cancer/oncogenic_oncolytic_legacy.png)

@caption

Topic 36: Cancer-Causing and Cancer-Killing Viruses

=== layout: two-panels

# 36. Phage Cycles: An Analogy

@left

![Lytic and lysogenic bacteriophage cycles](assets/set5_cancer/oncolytic_delivery_2025.png)

@right

- A lytic phage makes new particles and lyses its bacterial host.
- A temperate phage can maintain its genome in the host and later enter the lytic cycle.
- This is an analogy about persistence and lysis. Mammalian cancer-causing viruses and engineered oncolytic viruses have their own mechanisms.

=== layout: one-panel

# 36. Designing an Oncolytic Virus

@body

- Specificity can use a tumor receptor, tumor-active promoter, deleted viral function, or local delivery.
- Direct lysis can release tumor antigens and inflammatory signals, potentially recruiting immune responses.
- Test route, shedding, prior immunity, off-target infection, and systemic inflammation.

=== layout: one-panel

# 36. Evidence and Prevention

@body

- Human papillomavirus and hepatitis B virus are important prevention examples because vaccination can prevent the causal infection.
- A report of tumor regression after a natural infection is historically interesting, but it is not a controlled test of dose, safety, or cause.
- For an oncolytic candidate, report tumor type, route, dose, local and systemic safety, viral replication/shedding, immune effects, and a prespecified clinical endpoint.

@citation

NCI, Infectious Agents and Cancer: https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/causes-prevention/risk/infectious-agents

=== layout: subsection

# 37. Oncolytic-Virus Combinations

=== layout: two-panels

# 37. Why Combine an Oncolytic Virus?

@left

- Viral lysis releases tumor material; checkpoint inhibitors remove T-cell inhibitory signals.
- Why might that combination help in a tumor with too few active T cells?
- Which route, sequence, dosing, endpoint, biomarker, and safety measures must a study define?

@right

*Combination hypothesis*

oncolytic-virus infection

-> tumor-cell lysis and antigen release

-> immune-cell recruitment or priming

-> checkpoint blockade may allow activated T cells to keep working

=== layout: two-panels

# 37. Complementarity and Synergy

@left

## Different Jobs in a Combination

1. Virus reaches susceptible tumor cells.
2. Lysis releases tumor material.
3. Immune cells recognize and respond.
4. Checkpoint blockade can release inhibitory signals.

@right

- A plausible combination can fail: poor viral reach, T-cell exclusion, or toxicity can limit dosing.
- Radiation can alter antigen release and access; schedule changes benefit and harm.
- A valid comparison needs a control arm, not only a response after two treatments.

=== layout: two-panels

# 37. Building a Combination Study

@left

| Design element | Why it belongs in the protocol |
| --- | --- |
| Route and timing | determines exposure and sequence |
| Biomarkers | tests mechanism |
| Response definition | shrinkage, durability, survival |
| Safety monitoring | inflammation and organ toxicity |

@right

- A cold tumor may need more: access, antigenicity, and suppressive cells can limit response.
- Genes such as GM-CSF can be included in a virus to recruit or activate antigen-presenting cells, but the outcome remains an empirical question.

=== layout: one-panel

# 37. Claims a Result Can Support

@body

- *Supported:* in this defined tumor type, schedule, and comparison, the combination changed the measured response or safety outcome.
- *Not automatically supported:* the combination works for every cancer, the immune mechanism was the only cause, or an early response guarantees longer survival.
- For a later study slide, identify the population, virus, partner treatment, route, endpoint, toxicity plan, and biomarker before interpreting a headline.

@citation

NCI, Cancer Immunotherapy: https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/treatment/types/immunotherapy
